    "description": "Nestled at the end of a private, secluded and unspoilt lane in the 14th arrondissement, this exceptional 308m² artist’s studio-home offers a unique setting that blends history, art, architecture and nature.\n\nBuilt in the early 1900s, the property has been home to several major figures in 20th-century art, including the famous ceramist Georges Jouve, who lived there from 1945 to 1953, and the sculptor Émile Morlaix until 1990. Several of Morlaix’s works still remain in the garden today, silent witnesses to the artistic soul of the place.\n\nSheltered from view, the house opens onto a spectacular 450 m² landscaped garden, a true secret haven in the heart of Paris. A pond, sculptures, trees, stone terraces and views through the foliage create an almost surreal atmosphere. The silence here is absolute, and every room maintains a constant dialogue with nature.\n\nThe vast cathedral-style living room, soaring to a height of nearly 8 metres, forms the heart of the house. Flooded with light thanks to immense glass roofs and skylights, this spectacular reception area combines an atelier-style feel, modernist volumes and a relaxed lifestyle. \n\nThe ground floor comprises a reception area of over 80m² with an open-plan kitchen, a library lounge, a spacious study, an 18m² bedroom and a utility room. Upstairs, there is a 30m² master suite with a shower room, a study or fitness area overlooking the living room, a spacious walk-in wardrobe, a third bedroom of 20m² and a bathroom.\n\nAt the rear of the garden, a separate workshop naturally extends the property’s creative spirit. It could be used as a studio, a workspace, a guest house or a private gallery.\n\nA separate two-room duplex flat, with its own entrance and balcony, completes the property.\n\nFour private parking spaces in a private garage and a cellar are included with this exclusive property.\n\nIn the immediate vicinity of local shops, Parc Montsouris and transport links (Line 4 and RER B).\n\nA historic and deeply inspiring place, where you instantly forget you are in Paris and rediscover the atmosphere of a country house.",
